## Why This Is Asked

Rationale: session fixation is a common beginner pitfall when using server-side sessions. The plan demonstrates practical, verifiable steps and testing.

## Key Concepts

- Session fixation and rotation
- HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie flags
- Server-side session store (Redis)
- Per-user session invalidation
- Basic automated tests for session validity

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express-like sketch
app.post('/login', async (req,res)=>{
  const user = await authenticate(req.body);
  if(!user) return res.status(401).end();
  const sessionId = generateSessionId();
  await redis.set(`sess:${user.id}`, sessionId, 'EX', 3600);
  res.cookie('session', sessionId, { httpOnly: true, secure: true, sameSite: 'Strict' });
  res.json({ ok: true });
});

app.use((req,res,next)=>{
  const sid = req.cookies?.session;
  if(!sid) return res.status(401).end();
  // validate sid against Redis
  // deny if not found or mismatched
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this in a distributed setup with multiple app instances?
- How would you adapt for mobile clients that require cookie-less auth flows?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical security fundamentals at junior level with real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Rate limiting and lockout; constant-time comparisons; password hashing; MFA basics; secure logging/privacy.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo login flow illustrating bcrypt compare, MFA, and session issuance
async function login(req, res) {
  const { username, password, mfaToken } = req.body;
  const user = await getUser(username);
  if (!user) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  const ok = await bcrypt.compare(password, user.hash);
  if (!ok) return res.status(401).send('Invalid credentials');
  if (user.mfaEnabled) {
    if (!mfaToken) return res.status(401).send('MFA required');
    const verified = speakeasy.totp.verify({ secret: user.mfaSecret, encoding: 'base32', token: mfaToken });
    if (!verified) return res.status(401).send('Invalid MFA');
  }
  const session = createSession(user.id);
  res.cookie('session', session, { httpOnly: true, secure: true });
  return res.send({ ok: true });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for distributed rate limiting?
- How would you test lockout and recovery flows?
- How would you store and rotate MFA seeds securely?

## Why This Is Asked

Rationale: practical hardening of file uploads in common web stacks; beginner-friendly but requires concrete steps.

## Key Concepts

- File validation with MIME and content checks
- Storage isolation and permissions
- Sandboxing and least privilege
- Post-upload scanning and auditing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Express Multer config example
const upload = multer({
  dest: '/var/app/uploads',
  limits: { fileSize: 1024 * 1024 },
  fileFilter: (req, file, cb) => {
    const allowed = ['image/png','image/jpeg','application/pdf'];
    if (allowed.includes(file.mimetype)) cb(null, true);
    else cb(new Error('Invalid file type'), false);
  }
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle MIME spoofing and double extension tricks?
- How would you test security controls in CI/CD?
